finish('strongly-polynomial-linear-programming',
 id='TCS-0008',title='Does general rational linear programming have a strongly polynomial algorithm?',
 area='Optimization and mathematical programming',criterion='decision',question_type='yes_no',
 formal=r'''Does there exist a deterministic algorithm, with fixed polynomials p and q, that solves every rational linear program $\min\{c^Tx:Ax=b,\ x\ge0\}$, where A has m rows and n columns, using at most p(m+n) elementary arithmetic, comparison, and data-transfer operations, while every intermediate rational number has binary encoding length at most q(N)? Here N is the total input bit length. Solving means returning an exact optimal rational solution if one exists, or correctly reporting infeasibility or an objective unbounded below.''',
 definitions=r'''A is in Q^(m by n), b in Q^m, and c in Q^n, with m,n>=1. All coefficients are supplied explicitly as binary signed numerators and positive denominators. No full-rank, nondegeneracy, strict-feasibility, coefficient-size, or conditioning promise is made. Elementary scalar operations are addition, subtraction, multiplication, division by a nonzero quantity, comparisons, and transfers of stored values. The program is fixed and finite; no oracle, advice, randomization, floor operation, or uncharged access to binary digits is allowed. Rational encoding length means the bit length of a numerator-denominator representation. The polynomial p is independent of N and of every coefficient magnitude; q may depend on N. Consequently the algorithm must also admit a polynomial-time bit implementation. The output is a solution vector, not a required optimal basis. Infeasible means that the feasible set is empty; unbounded means it is nonempty and the infimum of c^T x is minus infinity.''',
 answer_criterion='Construct and prove correct an algorithm meeting both the dimension-only operation bound and polynomial intermediate bit lengths for every input, or prove that no such algorithm exists. A bound depending on coefficient length or a condition number is insufficient unless that dependence is eliminated. A lower bound for one pivot rule or one class of interior-point trajectories does not establish nonexistence of all strongly polynomial algorithms.',
 context=[
  paragraph('Linear programming is already solvable in polynomial time when input length includes the bits used to write its coefficients. The unresolved issue is whether the number of arithmetic decisions can depend only on the number of constraints and variables. Very large capacities, costs, or denominators may require long arithmetic operands, but the question asks whether they must also force more arithmetic steps.', 'topp'),
  paragraph('Both requirements in the definition are essential. Bounding the number of steps while allowing intermediate integers with exponentially many bits could hide enormous computation inside single arithmetic operations. Bounding only total bit time gives ordinary polynomial-time linear programming, which is already known. The strongly polynomial target combines a dimension-only operation count with controlled numerical representation.', 'two'),
  paragraph('Combinatorial optimization supplies positive examples. Tardos’s method removes dependence on the magnitudes of the right-hand side and objective, and yields strongly polynomial bounds for suitable small-coefficient constraint matrices. More recently, a strongly polynomial algorithm was obtained when each row, or each column, has at most two nonzero entries. These cases show that large numerical data alone need not force many arithmetic steps.', 'tardos'),
  paragraph('For unrestricted matrices, an algorithm may need a different way to navigate the feasible region. Long central paths can defeat broad classes of path-following methods, even though other approaches remain possible. A separate status issue is a 2025 preprint, revised in July 2026, claiming a general solution. Its claim is recorded below, but this review has not established its correctness or found independent validation.', 'claim')],
 why='This isolates whether the combinatorial dimensions of a linear program suffice to control its arithmetic complexity. A resolution would clarify the relation between numerical precision, polyhedral structure, and the complexity of one of optimization’s basic models.',
 importance=dict(score=97,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-10',reason='A leading open question in optimization, separating ordinary polynomial-time solvability from a dimension-only arithmetic bound. Its reach includes general linear programming and many combinatorial optimization problems.'),
 progress=[
  step('1979–1984','The ellipsoid method and early interior-point algorithms establish polynomial-time solvability in the total input bit length. They do not provide a general strongly polynomial operation bound.','topp'),
  step('1986','Tardos gives an algorithm whose arithmetic count is independent of the right-hand side and objective magnitudes, with remaining dependence on the constraint matrix. Small integer entries in that matrix give important strongly polynomial special cases.','tardos'),
  step('2022','Allamigeon, Gaubert, and Vandame construct LP families forcing exponentially many iterations for the self-concordant-barrier path-following methods covered by their theorem. This restricts a route to solving the problem; it is not an unrestricted LP lower bound.','barrier'),
  step('2024','Dadush, Koh, Natura, Olver, and Végh solve minimum-cost generalized flow and LPs with at most two nonzeros per row or column in strongly polynomial time. Their analysis also controls intermediate bit lengths.','two'),
  step('2026-06','Dadush, Ma, Natura, and Végh improve interior-point bounds in terms of straight-line complexity and give strongly polynomial trust-region subroutines. Straight-line complexity can itself be large, so these are not dimension-only bounds for arbitrary LPs.','trust'),
  step('2026-07-04','Version 10 of Awoniyi’s preprint claims at most 2(m+n) complementary-pivot iterations for general LP. This review has not validated the termination/correctness argument and complete bit-complexity guarantee, and found no independent primary confirmation. The claim is not promoted to an established resolution.','claim')],
 references=[
  ref('topp','Problem 8: Linear Programming: Strongly Polynomial?','The Open Problems Project; problem attributed to Nimrod Megiddo',2010,'https://topp.openproblem.net/p8','Statement and historical results; entry last revised 12 March 2010'),
  ref('tardos','A Strongly Polynomial Algorithm to Solve Combinatorial Linear Programs','Éva Tardos',1986,'https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.34.2.250','Operations Research 34(2), 250–256'),
  ref('two','A strongly polynomial algorithm for linear programs with at most two non-zero entries per row or column','Daniel Dadush; Zhuan Khye Koh; Bento Natura; Neil Olver; László A. Végh',2024,'https://homepages.cwi.nl/~dadush/papers/genflow.pdf','Author manuscript, Theorem 1.1 and Section 1.3 on computation models; STOC 2024 DOI 10.1145/3618260.3649764'),
  ref('barrier','No self-concordant barrier interior point method is strongly polynomial','Xavier Allamigeon; Stéphane Gaubert; Nicolas Vandame',2022,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.02186','STOC 2022; lower bounds for the specified path-following framework'),
  ref('trust','Trust Region Interior Point Methods: Optimal l2- and Faster Wide-Neighborhood Path Following','Daniel Dadush; Haoyuan Ma; Bento Natura; László A. Végh',2026,'https://homepages.cwi.nl/~dadush/papers/trust-region.pdf','Abstract and Section 1; STOC 2026 DOI 10.1145/3798129.3800790'),
  ref('claim','A strongly polynomial-time algorithm for the general linear programming problem','Samuel Awoniyi',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.12041v10','Unverified claim; version 10, 4 July 2026, Sections 4, 6, and 7; originally submitted March 2025')],
 status='uncertain',status_note='The standard problem remains listed as open, but a direct claimed solution exists in Awoniyi’s July 2026 revision. This card records it without treating it as established: correctness, the iteration bound, and the full strongly polynomial guarantee have not been independently validated in this review. Checked through 10 September 2026.',
 formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
